/* The handoff's fixed-height column: header fixed, content the only flexible
 * region, CTA row and footer note pinned under it.
 *
 * THREE RAW LENGTHS HERE ARE DELIBERATE, and they are the handoff's own. The
 * README's "Spacing, radius, elevation" section prescribes header padding
 * `8px 14px 10px`, and its navigation model prescribes a `34px` rounded-square
 * back button. 14, 10 and 34 are not members of the spacing scale Tokens.uss
 * holds - the steps either side are 12 and 16, and 8 - so tokenising them
 * would mean either inventing three one-use tokens or rounding the handoff to
 * the nearest step it did not ask for. Theme.uss already settled this
 * convention for the same class of value: `Button` carries a raw 12px padding
 * and a raw 3px drop edge, TextField a raw 10px, `.divider` a raw 1px, and
 * `icons.uss` the raw 19px glyph box. The token layer holds the SCALE - the
 * spacing steps, the radii, the colours, the type - and one-off component
 * geometry quoted from the handoff is written literally, next to the sentence
 * it came from. Every value below that IS on the scale uses its token.
 */
.screen-scaffold { flex-grow: 1; flex-direction: column; }

/* 8px 14px 10px, straight from the handoff. */
.screen-scaffold__header {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    padding-left: 14px;
    padding-right: 14px;
}

/* The 34px rounded square at the header's left edge, per the handoff's
 * navigation model. Radius is --radius-tile (11px), inside the handoff's
 * 9-14px icon-tile band and its own value on `Wave Defense.dc.html`.
 *
 * PADDING AND BLOCK MARGINS ARE ZEROED, and that is not tidying. Theme.uss
 * gives every Button 12px of vertical and 16px of horizontal padding plus a
 * 4px top and bottom margin, for the CTA shape. Inherited onto a fixed 34px
 * square that leaves a 2x10px content box, and the 19px glyph inside it is an
 * in-flow child laid out in exactly that box - so the chevron would be
 * crushed to a sliver, and the button would sit 4px low against a title that
 * has no such margin. The radius is overridden for the same reason: a nav
 * affordance is not a CTA and must not read as one.
 *
 * THE FILL AND THE BORDER ARE NOT HERE ANY MORE, AND THAT IS THE `pill-back`
 * CHANGE. They were `--surface-sunk` with no border - the affordance sunk
 * INTO the paper. Every pushed screen in the handoff lifts it off instead:
 * white, with a `0 1px 3px` shadow that USS answers with a hairline. Both
 * now come from Theme.uss's `.pill-back`, which this element carries in the
 * UXML, so a screen wanting the same white tile for a pause control or a
 * speed pill reads one class rather than re-deriving two values. */
.screen-scaffold__back {
    width: 34px;
    height: 34px;
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 0;
    padding-right: 0;
    border-radius: var(--radius-tile);
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* THE CTA PRESS DOES NOT BELONG ON A NAV CONTROL, and until this rule it was
 * on one. Theme.uss's `Button:active` is `type + pseudo-class` (0,1,1) and
 * every rule above is a single class (0,1,0), so a tap on the back chevron
 * flooded a white 34px tile with --violet-deep and dropped it 2px - the
 * physical CTA press, on the one control in the header that is not a CTA.
 * bible 10.6 asks for that press on PRIMARY CTAs specifically. Two classes
 * and the pseudo-class (0,2,1) is what outranks it deterministically.
 * The tile still answers a touch: --violet-tint under the chevron is the
 * handoff's own pressed treatment for a secondary control. */
.screen-scaffold__back.pill-back:active {
    translate: 0 0;
    background-color: var(--violet-tint);
}

/* --mute-soft is icons.uss's default tint, set for a nav glyph sitting on the
 * --surface fill of the tab bar. This chevron sits on a white tile of its
 * own, so it takes the handoff's own `stroke="#6b5fa8"` - the back affordance
 * is a primary control on a pushed screen, not tertiary chrome, and violet is
 * what says "this one acts" in a header whose title is --ink. */
.screen-scaffold__back .icon {
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: var(--violet-text);
}

/* THE TITLE BLOCK IS A COLUMN NOW, NOT A LABEL, so the eyebrow can sit over
 * the title in the handoff's header - `Gene Lab` over `Splicing Chamber`,
 * `Hollow Reach · defense` over `Wave 7`. The column takes the flex-grow the
 * title used to carry, and the title gives it up: with both growing, a
 * one-line title stretched to the header's full height and the eyebrow rode
 * up off the cap line. */
.screen-scaffold__titles { flex-grow: 1; flex-direction: column; }
.screen-scaffold__title { flex-grow: 0; }

/* HIDDEN BY DEFAULT, AND REMOVAL IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE. `SectionCard`'s
 * heading and the back chevron are REMOVED when they have nothing to say,
 * because a `display: none` node is still a node a screen reader walks. This
 * one is hidden instead: the eyebrow is re-settable after construction (the
 * `Eyebrow` property; Task 16's chamber sets it from a model), so removing
 * it would mean rebuilding and re-inserting a label at a known index inside
 * the column every time a screen changed its mind. The scaffold already
 * hides its footer note for the same reason and by the same mechanism.
 *
 * `letter-spacing` IS RESTATED although `.t-micro` sets it, because a screen
 * that swaps the eyebrow's type class must not silently lose the handoff's
 * `.1em` tracking - it is what makes a 10px uppercase line read as a label
 * rather than as small text. */
.screen-scaffold__eyebrow {
    color: var(--mute);
    letter-spacing: 1px;
    display: none;
}

.screen-scaffold__header-slot { flex-direction: row; align-items: center; }

/* The handoff's resource readout - `Splice Chamber.dc.html` puts the charge
 * counter here as `background: #ffffff; border-radius: 999px; padding: 6px
 * 12px 6px 7px`, a tinted glyph disc on the left and a muted cap on the
 * right ("4" then "/5").
 *
 * 17px IS HALF OF THE HANDOFF'S 34px, and the 34 is arithmetic rather than
 * a guess: 6px padding, a 22px disc, 6px padding. This element measures 36
 * because UI Toolkit's box is border-box and the hairline standing in for
 * the handoff's shadow adds 1px top and bottom - so 17 is one UNDER half.
 * That is the safe direction and the only safe direction: Tokens.uss's
 * radius block has the 50-bar measurement, and the short form is that below
 * half the cap flattens slightly and above half it degrades without bound.
 * If the disc, the padding or the border moves, re-measure and change this
 * with it.
 *
 * THE ASYMMETRIC PADDING IS THE HANDOFF'S AND IS NOT A TYPO. The disc is
 * round and already carries its own visual inset, so 7px on its side against
 * 12px on the text's side is what makes the two ends look equally padded. */
.resource-pill {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    background-color: var(--surface);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--hairline);
    border-radius: 17px;
    padding-top: 6px;
    padding-bottom: 6px;
    padding-left: 7px;
    padding-right: var(--space-3);
}

/* 11px is half of the 22px disc - a square element, so both axes clamp to
 * the same number and this is a true circle (Theme.uss header item 4). The
 * tint is the handoff's #dcf0f8, which is --teal-tint to within 15 in summed
 * channel distance; the charge counter is the only resource pill the phase
 * builds and the handoff draws its glyph teal. A pill for a violet or amber
 * resource needs a modifier here rather than a second component. */
.resource-pill__disc {
    width: 22px;
    height: 22px;
    border-radius: 11px;
    background-color: var(--teal-tint);
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-right: 7px;
}

/* TWO CLASSES, DELIBERATELY. `.icon` in icons.uss sizes every glyph at 19px
 * and tints it --mute-soft, and a single-class rule here would tie with it
 * on specificity and be settled by whichever sheet the panel happened to
 * apply last. The handoff draws this one at 12px in a 22px disc. Every
 * override of a Theme/icons class in this project's component sheets is
 * written with two classes for the same reason. */
.resource-pill .resource-pill__glyph {
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: var(--teal-text);
}

/* THE VALUE IS BIGGER THAN THE 11px FLOOR, AND THAT IS THE ONE PLACE THIS
 * PROJECT OVERRIDES `.t-num`'s SIZE. StatCell.uss declined to, for one pixel
 * (11 against the handoff's 12) - "the floor wins; the 1px is recorded here
 * rather than argued with silently". This is six pixels: the handoff sets
 * this readout at 15px, and it is the only number in the header. Rendering
 * it at 11 would make the charge count the same size as the "/5" after it.
 * The floor is a MINIMUM, so going up does not touch bible 10.6 - what the
 * floor forbids is --text-micro, which verify-uss-tokens.sh check 2 reads.
 *
 * 15px IS A LITERAL because the type scale does not hold it: --text-card-title
 * is 13 and --text-cta is 17, either side. It is inline geometry quoted from
 * `Splice Chamber.dc.html`, on this file's own convention. */
.resource-pill .resource-pill__value {
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--ink);
}

/* "/5" - the cap, at --text-secondary and --mute-soft, which is the
 * handoff's #b3adc2 exactly. It stays on `.t-num` because it IS a numeral
 * and would otherwise fall back to the body face's non-tabular digits. */
.resource-pill .resource-pill__suffix {
    color: var(--mute-soft);
}

/* The one flexible region, and the only one that scrolls. The three rows
 * around it are flex-shrink: 0 so a tall screen scrolls its body rather than
 * compressing its chrome - the handoff's "content is the only flexible
 * region", made structural rather than left to whatever each screen's content
 * happens to measure. */
/* THE GUTTER IS ON THE CONTENT CONTAINER, NOT ON THE ScrollView ITSELF, AND
 * THAT IS ABOUT THE SHADOW RATHER THAN ABOUT THE SPACING. Both put a card's
 * edge at 12px; only one leaves the drop shadow anywhere to fall.
 *
 * A ScrollView clips its content at its VIEWPORT, and padding on the
 * ScrollView root pushes that viewport inward - so with the gutter here, the
 * clip edge and the card edge are the same 12px line, and `.elev-1`'s
 * wrapper (which reaches 6px further out on each side, by negative margin, so
 * the surface lands on the gutter) is cut off exactly where its ink starts.
 * Measured on LineageView before this change: the card surface at x=12 with
 * x=6..11 pure --paper, no ink at all, while the same SectionCard on
 * CodexSheet - a sheet, no scaffold, no ScrollView - showed its falloff at
 * x=12..15 at 1-3/255. Same component, same rule, two containers.
 *
 * Moving the padding one level down leaves the viewport spanning the full
 * 430 and clips at the frame edge instead, which is where a phone clips
 * anyway. The handoff's shadow is `0 2px 8px` - no horizontal offset and an
 * 8px blur - so it HAS a side falloff by design, and a card against the
 * gutter is exactly where it shows.
 *
 * `unity-scroll-view__content-container` IS UNITY'S OWN CLASS ON THAT
 * ELEMENT, and it is the documented USS hook for it rather than a name read
 * off an inspector. Verified by measurement rather than by trust: if the
 * selector matched nothing, the gutter would be gone entirely and every card
 * would sit at x=0 - which is a louder failure than a subtly wrong inset. */
.screen-scaffold__content {
    flex-grow: 1;
}

/* `min-height: 100%` IS WHAT MAKES THE CONTENT REGION FILLABLE, AND WITHOUT
 * IT NO SCREEN'S `flex-grow` DOES ANYTHING.
 *
 * A ScrollView's content container is sized BY ITS CONTENT - that is how it
 * can exceed the viewport and have something to scroll - so a child carrying
 * `flex-grow: 1` had no free space to take and every short screen ended in a
 * band of bare --paper below its last card. Measured at about 180px on
 * founder naming, where the handoff's own hero is `flex: 1; min-height:
 * 300px` (Onboarding.dc.html:43) and absorbs exactly that slack.
 *
 * THE PERCENTAGE RESOLVES AGAINST THE VIEWPORT, NOT THE SCREEN. This
 * element's parent is Unity's `unity-content-viewport`, which is
 * `flex-grow: 1` inside a ScrollView that is itself `flex-grow: 1` in the
 * scaffold's column - so 100% is "as tall as the visible region", which is
 * the number a screen wanting to fill it actually means.
 *
 * IT DOES NOT STOP ANYTHING SCROLLING. A MINIMUM is not a height: content
 * taller than the viewport still sizes the container past 100% and still
 * scrolls, which the Scaffold fixture's short frame is the standing check on.
 *
 * TEN SCREENS SEE THIS AND ONLY ONE MOVES. A taller container changes no
 * pixel unless a direct child of `Content` carries `flex-grow`, because the
 * column is `justify-content: flex-start` and this element has no fill of its
 * own. The founder screen's hero is the only such child in the project; the
 * corpus was re-captured in full to say so rather than to assume it. It used
 * to be a `SectionCard` carrying `.founder-naming-view__hero`; as of Task 14c
 * it is a `HeroBand` and the `flex-grow` is written by `HeroBand.Fill`, so
 * the child that grows is now any band a screen calls `Fill` on.
 *
 * NOT `height: 100%`. That would CAP the container at the viewport and clip
 * every screen whose content is taller - the roster, the chamber, the
 * Scaffold fixture's own short frame - which is the failure this rule is one
 * character away from. */
.screen-scaffold__content .unity-scroll-view__content-container {
    padding-left: var(--gutter);
    padding-right: var(--gutter);
    min-height: 100%;
}

.screen-scaffold__cta-row {
    flex-direction: column;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding-left: var(--gutter);
    padding-right: var(--gutter);
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
}

/* A SENTENCE, SO IT WRAPS, AND IT SITS ON THE GUTTER LIKE EVERYTHING ELSE.
 * Screens hand their caveats to this row - RosterView deleted its own
 * `__notice` rule on the understanding that the colour, size, wrap and margin
 * were all covered here. Two of those were not. Without `white-space: normal`
 * a long note ("Your roster could not be loaded, so this is not all of it.
 * Try again.") renders on one unwrapped line, and with no horizontal padding
 * it runs edge to edge, because `.screen-host`'s gutter was removed in the
 * same branch that moved the note here. Every other multi-sentence Label in
 * the project sets `white-space: normal`; this was the one that did not. The
 * corpus never showed it: the only footer note in any fixture is short and
 * centred, and the roster fixture is complete, which hides this one. */
.screen-scaffold__footer-note {
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    white-space: normal;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    color: var(--mute);
    padding-left: var(--gutter);
    padding-right: var(--gutter);
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
}
